{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1090,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you ask someone to draw a star, they'll\nprobably draw something like this - or this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6010,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or this. Even ignoring the rainbows, this\ndoesn't seem very scientific, since we know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10970,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars are actually big hot ROUND balls of\nplasma and far enough away that they're basically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15920,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just dots. So why do we draw stars that have\npoints?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20020,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is surprisingly simple: we see\nstars as pointy. Look carefully next time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25320,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're outside on a dark night - or just look\nat this dot (it works best if you make the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29369,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video fullscreen, close one eye, and relax\nthe other as if you're looking at something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33500,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far away). You should see a pointy, star-like\nshape! In fact, it's not just humans that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38710,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see pointy stars - some telescopes see them\nthat way, too!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42460,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is all because light is a wave. When\nlight from a distant source passes through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46860,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an opening or around an object, its waves\nare bounced or bent slightly and interfere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51360,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each other, so the passing light picks\nup an imprint of that opening or object. A"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55910,
    "dDurationMs": 4309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straight line (whether it's a slit letting\nlight through or a rod blocking the light)"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60219,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaves its imprint by spreading the light\nout into a perpendicular series of dashes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64059,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(- like what you see when you squint!) A cross\ncreates two, perpendicular, sets of dashes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69130,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circles cause concentric rings, squares spawn\na kind of dashed four-pointed star, hexagons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74969,
    "dDurationMs": 6341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dashed six-pointed stars; and the famous double\nslit experiment gives a series of dashed dashes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81310,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "My favorite diffraction pattern, though, is\nprobably that of the Penrose tiling - it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85939,
    "dDurationMs": 5691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply gorgeous… not that you see Penrose-tiling-shaped\nopenings very often. But… the point of all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91630,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these imprints is that they're the result\nof a point of light being spread out when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95329,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viewed through a particular opening or past\na particular object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98899,
    "dDurationMs": 4371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the Hubble space telescope has\nfour struts that support its small secondary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103270,
    "dDurationMs": 5409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mirror, and their imprint causes the 4-pointed\nstars in hubble photos. And I bet you can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108679,
    "dDurationMs": 4021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guess the shape of the aperture on the lens\nthat took this picture. Similarly, the lenses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112700,
    "dDurationMs": 4809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our eyes have subtle structural imperfections\ncalled suture lines where the fibers that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117509,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make up the lens meet. These imperfections\nleave a very particular imprint on light as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122529,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it passes by, as researchers have confirmed\nby shining lasers in people’s eyes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127079,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, even though stars themselves are just\ntiny round dots, by the time the light reaches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131670,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our retina, it's been smeared out into a starlike\nshape. Every single eye on earth will see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137120,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a slightly different starlike smear depending\non the exact nature of its suture lines - even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142750,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your left and right eyes will differ! What's\nweird, though, is that any particular eye"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147379,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sees the same shape for every star - so while\nit is scientifically acceptable to draw stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153159,
    "dDurationMs": 3881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like this, if you draw more than one in a\nsingle picture, you better make sure they're"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157040,
    "dDurationMs": 1509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the exact same shape!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158549,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On top of that, since diffraction spreads\nlonger wavelength red light out more than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162260,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bluer light, the arms of these star-shapes\nare actually mini-rainbows with red on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166920,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside and blue in the middle! Which, again,\nyou can see in hubble photographs or if you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171769,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look even more carefully at a single point\nof light. So as crazy as it sounds, coloring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176390,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in stars with rainbows is super scientifically\naccurate - as long as the colors go the right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188319,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way."
    } ]
  } ]
}
